INT. PLANETARIUM - CENTRAL DOME - NIGHT

A great hemisphere of dark. In its middle, a squat projector shaped like a spiked beetle, squatting on a platform ringed with reclining seats. The cupola above glows faintly with the residue of the day's last show.

Soft footfalls. Someone is breathing carefully, the way people do when they have broken into a place and wish to be forgiven for it.

PERSEPHONE "PIP" ADEYEMI-LARKIN, 30s, small, black thermal jacket, a miner's headlamp turned to red. She kneels at the base of the projector with a canvas tool roll, unwrapping picks the way a surgeon lays out scalpels. Her face is composed. Her eyes are not.

She whispers into her sleeve.

PIP
I'm at the base. Panel is off. I see the vault door.

She waits. Nothing.

PIP (CONT'D)
Anyone?

She sighs. Presses her palm to the dome's cold metal. Tilts her head to look up through the beetle's legs into the dark of a ceiling she cannot see.

The planetarium, empty, is still a place that holds its breath.

She slides a pick into the lock. Turns it a quarter. Listens. Turns it back. Wipes sweat from her upper lip with the heel of her glove.

PIP (CONT'D)
Come on, old girl.

Click. A heavy tumbler falls somewhere inside. She holds her breath. Another. Another. The door drifts open an inch.

Behind her, a SOUND. Soft. The slide of a shoe.

She does not turn. Her hand drops to the tool roll, closes on a flat steel bar.

A VOICE from the dark.

VOICE (O.S.)
That lock was installed in 1958. It sticks on the third tumbler.

Pip pivots, bar raised.

A tall figure steps from between two seats. Silver hair. Cardigan. Pocket flashlight in one hand, thermos in the other.

DR. MORDECAI ASHFORD-LUND, 70s, lowers the flashlight so the beam falls on the floor between them. He is calm. Infinitely tired.

Now she knows him. She lowers the bar one inch.

PIP
Doctor.

MORDECAI
Miss Adeyemi-Larkin. You came earlier than I thought.

PIP
You knew.

MORDECAI
I wrote the alarm schedule. I turned it off at nine.

PIP
Why?

He sets the thermos on a seat. Sits beside it. Sighs.

MORDECAI
Because forty years of children have lain under that sky, and not one of them has ever asked me what's inside the projector.

PIP
What's inside the projector?

MORDECAI
The Hope Diamond, as it happens.

PIP
(a beat)
That's a joke.

MORDECAI
It is not a joke. It is a stone the museum board acquired in 1982 and kept off the books. I have guarded it for forty years in the one place no thief would ever think to look.

PIP
Except me.

MORDECAI
Except you.

She looks at the open vault. At the old man. At the bar in her hand.

PIP
Why would you turn off the alarm, then?

MORDECAI
(simply)
Because I'd like somebody to see it before I die.

He rises. Takes the flashlight in two fingers and clicks it off.

MORDECAI (CONT'D)
Open it, Pip. I will turn on the stars.

He crosses to the control panel. Presses a button.

The dome erupts into a billion pinpoints of light.

Pip stands in the center, bar forgotten, the vault door swinging wide. In the dark inside the beetle, something catches the starlight and throws it back, a single cold blue fire.

PIP
(breathing)
Mordecai.

MORDECAI
Yes?

PIP
You should have asked sooner.

He smiles, and for a moment the whole dome is theirs.
